  "assumptions": [
    "Illustration: a made-up two-setting loss bowl with simulated batch noise, not a real model trained on real data.",
    "Loss = (2.4 x² + 0.6 z²)/2, drawn at half its value as a surface with contours. Steep along x (left-right), so overshoot shows there first: x swings grow once learning rate × 2.4 > 2, i.e. above about 0.83.",
    "Step = −learning rate × (true slope + noise). The noise is random, centred on zero, with size 1.5·√((64−b)/(63·b)) per axis for batch size b, so it is exactly zero at 64. One step lowers the loss only on average.",
    "The purple arrow is the next mini-batch step (the way the ball will move). The dark arrow is the full-data step, against the true slope. Both are drawn 3× their true length, with no cap, so steps in a diverging run look very long.",
    "A run ends after 40 steps (finished), or as diverged when the loss goes over 5 or the ball leaves the drawn bowl. Diverged points are drawn at the rim. Ready means no step has been taken yet.",
    "The host steps 30 times a second and the ball updates on every 4th tick (7.5 updates per second). The trail is the start point plus one point per update (at most 40). The ghost keeps the last run.",
    "Noise values come from a fixed seed, so Reset replays the same sequence. A new run uses the next values. Changing a setting mid-run affects the steps that follow."
  ]
}